# 2026-11-17 17:02:24-05:00 - Announcing Peaseblossom: an Oberon-2 compiler, and libraries and programs for it and for voc

This software was developed with the aid of AI.

I'm announcing a set of Oberon-2 projects: a compiler, libraries, and programs built with them. All are on GitHub, and the software is under the BSD 3-Clause License.

## For voc (Vishap Oberon)

### olibfyaml

A binding to libfyaml, the YAML 1.2 parser and emitter. It covers parsing, navigation, typed values, building and emitting YAML or JSON, anchors, tags and source locations. Errors in the input come back as values with file:line:column messages.

=> https://github.com/tkurtbond/olibfyaml olibfyaml
=> https://github.com/pantoniou/libfyaml libfyaml

## For both voc and poc

### opp

The Oberon pretty printer. It reformats modules within a maximum line length and keeps every comment. It handles both compilers' language extensions. Before writing, it checks that its output has exactly the tokens and comments of its input.

=> https://github.com/tkurtbond/opp opp

### Ropes

Ropes, strings represented as trees of pieces, with a self-test and a command-line demo.

=> https://github.com/tkurtbond/oberon-ropes Ropes

### ArgParser

Command-line parsing: long and short options, clustered short options, option values, commands, and usage messages.

=> https://github.com/tkurtbond/oberon-argparser ArgParser

## Peaseblossom

### poc

The Peaseblossom Oberon Compiler: an Oberon-2 compiler with an LLVM backend for Linux, FreeBSD, OpenBSD and NetBSD, and a VAX backend for VAX/VMS 5.5-2. It was motivated in large part by my wish for a more modern language than the limited ones available on the VAX system I maintain. A release tarball needs only clang and GNU make, or it can be bootstrapped using Vishap Oberon. Packages are available for Fedora, Debian, FreeBSD, OpenBSD and NetBSD. It comes with a User's Guide and a Reference Guide.

=> https://github.com/tkurtbond/peaseblossom poc (Peaseblossom)

### polibfyaml

olibfyaml for poc, with the same API, tests and examples.

=> https://github.com/tkurtbond/polibfyaml polibfyaml

ova_fmt_po, an example program using polibfyaml: it formats characters for the OVA tabletop RPG from YAML as reStructuredText, gemtext, Markdown and more.

=> https://github.com/tkurtbond/ova_fmt_po ova_fmt_po

### pofltk

A thick binding to FLTK 1.4. FLTK's classes are record types a program extends, and draw() and handle() are type-bound procedures it overrides. Coverage includes widgets, layout (flex and grid), menus, dialogs, text editors, browsers, trees, tables, images, the clipboard and preferences. FlTest drives a program's windows in tests on Xvfb or a headless compositor. Many of FLTK's test programs are ported as examples.

=> https://github.com/tkurtbond/pofltk pofltk
=> https://www.fltk.org FLTK

ova_cg_po, an example program using pofltk: a character generator for OVA. It's a complete program with a menu bar, tabs, grids, file choosers, preferences and window tests. Its tour for pofltk readers says where in its code each technique is.

=> https://github.com/tkurtbond/ova_cg_po ova_cg_po
=> https://github.com/tkurtbond/ova_cg_po/blob/main/doc/pofltk-tour.md ova_cg_po: a tour for pofltk readers

### poDateTime

Dates, times, durations and time zones. It has instants, the proleptic Gregorian calendar, clocks, POSIX TZ rules and tz database files, ISO 8601, RFC 3339 and strftime formats. It runs on the four Unixes and on VAX/VMS.

=> https://github.com/tkurtbond/poDateTime poDateTime

### pounix

Unix services that poc's runtime doesn't provide: dropping root for good, file descriptors and directories, running child processes with an argument list (no shell), and errno.

=> https://github.com/tkurtbond/pounix pounix

### posockets

TCP (IPv4 and IPv6) and Unix-domain sockets. It works with poc's cooperative Tasks, so one thread can serve many connections, with deadlines.

=> https://github.com/tkurtbond/posockets posockets

### polibtls

A binding to libtls (LibreTLS on Linux, FreeBSD and NetBSD; OpenBSD's own). It supports server contexts with several key pairs, client certificates, and non-blocking I/O with Tasks. It's tested against openssl, gnutls-cli and Go's TLS.

=> https://github.com/tkurtbond/polibtls polibtls

### pogemini

A Gemini server built on the four libraries above. It serves several capsules by host name, each with its own certificate, and can require client certificates for parts of a capsule. Every connection runs in a task in one thread.

=> https://github.com/tkurtbond/pogemini pogemini

### poDB

A database-independent SQL interface, with drivers for SQLite and PostgreSQL. A program moves from one to the other by changing its connection URL and the driver it imports.

=> https://github.com/tkurtbond/poDB poDB

Comments, bug reports and patches are welcome. Many of the poc libraries are early in their lives; polibfyaml and pofltk have had the most use.
